      "name": "StartClientReceiveHandler",
      "signature": "undefined4 StartClientReceiveHandler(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Initiates client network receive thread and handles initialization result.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Call NET_StartClientReceiveThread() to start receiving client network data\n2. Evaluate thread start result:\n   - If result == 0 (critical error): Store error flag and shutdown\n   - If result == 2 (partial failure): Store warning flag and shutdown\n3. Store appropriate control handler state in g_aPendingControlHandlers[0x40]\n4. Call StopMessageLoop() to terminate message processing loop in error cases\n5. Return 1 to indicate handler completion\n\nParameters:\n  (none - no parameters)\n\nReturns:\n  uint: Always returns 1 (indicates handler executed successfully)\n        Return value does not indicate network thread success/failure\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Thread creation error (return 0): Immediate application shutdown\n  - Partial failure (return 2): Also triggers message loop termination\n  - Success (return 1 or 3): Handler completes normally\n\nError Handling:\n  Network initialization failures trigger StopMessageLoop() which terminates\n  the application's main event loop, causing graceful shutdown.\n\nDependencies:\n  - NET_StartClientReceiveThread: Initiates network receive thread\n  - StopMessageLoop: Terminates application event loop\n  - g_aPendingControlHandlers: Global control state array (256 uint elements)\npfnTickCallback parameter of MessagePumpLoop\n",
      "name_source": "Classic/1.00",
      "method": "NOP",
