      "name": "SaveGameStateConfiguration",
      "signature": "undefined4 SaveGameStateConfiguration(undefined4 param_1, int param_2)",
      "calling_convention": "__fastcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Saves game state configuration data to a file in the save directory.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Retrieve save directory path into szPath buffer (1024 bytes)\n2. Construct save filename in szFilename buffer (500 bytes) using format string\n3. Open file with CreateFileWrapper: GENERIC_WRITE access, CREATE_ALWAYS mode, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL\n4. Return failure (0) if file open fails\n5. Write 0x82 (130) bytes from global DAT_6fa97cc0 (config header)\n6. If dwSize is non-zero, write dwSize additional bytes from pConfigData buffer\n7. Close file handle and return success (1)\n\nParameters:\npConfigData (RCX/ECX): Pointer to additional config data to write, or NULL if only writing header\ndwSize (RDX/EDX): Number of bytes to write from pConfigData; if 0, only header is written\n\nReturns:\nEAX: BOOL success flag (1 = success, 0 = file creation failed)\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If dwSize is 0, only the 0x82-byte header from DAT_6fa97cc0 is written\n- File is opened with CREATE_ALWAYS, overwriting any existing file\n- Both write operations share same dwBytesWritten output parameter\n- Error handling only checks file creation; write failures are not validated\n\nMagic Numbers:\n0x82 (130): Header size in bytes to write from DAT_6fa97cc0\n0x80 (128): Maximum size of additional config data (dwSize parameter)\n0x400 (1024): Path buffer size\n0x8000000: FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL flag value for CreateFileWrapper\n0x40000000: GENERIC_WRITE flag value for CreateFileWrapper\n\nGlobal Data:\nDAT_6fa97cc0: 130-byte config header buffer (written first to all save files)\n\nAPI Calls:\nGetSaveDirectoryPath: Fills szPath with save game directory\nFUN_6fa8547c: Constructs filename using format string\nCreateFileWrapper: Opens file (wrapper around Windows CreateFileA)\nWriteFileWrapper: Writes buffer to file (wrapper around Windows WriteFile)\nCloseHandleWrapper: Closes file handle (wrapper around Windows CloseHandle)",
