      "comment": "Parse a date format string and output formatted date/time values.\n\n  Algorithm:\n  1. Loop through each character in the format string\n  2. For each character, count consecutive occurrences of that character\n  3. Based on the character and repeat count, determine output format:\n     - 'd' (day): output as 'd', 'a' (1-digit), 'A' (zero-padded), 'a' (2-digit), 'A' (zero-padded)\n     - 'm' (month): output as 'm', 'b', 'B' (zero-padded variant)\n     - 'y' (year): output as 'y', 'Y' (zero-padded)\n     - 'h'/'H' (hour): output as 'h', 'I' (zero-padded)\n     - 's'/'S' (seconds): output as 's', 'S' (zero-padded)\n     - 't' (timezone): loads hour-based AM/PM string (offset 0x98 if < 0xc, else 0x9c)\n     - 'a'/'A' (am/pm): matches 'am/pm' or 'a.m./p.m.' string\n     - '\\'' (quote): outputs literal characters until closing quote\n     - other: output character as-is if 0x80 classification bit set, else output raw\n  4. Set g_dwCurrentCharSeparator flag based on repeat count\n  5. Call ProcessDateFormatSpecifier for actual output formatting\n  6. Continue until null terminator or buffer exhausted\n\n  Parameters:\n    pszFormatString:  Pointer to format string with format specifiers\n    nSpecCount:       Integer parameter count or temp storage (repurposed in loop)\n    pbBuffer:         Pointer to output buffer (double-indirect, points to write position)\n    pcbRemaining:     Pointer to remaining buffer size in bytes (decremented on write)\n    nContext:         Base address of context structure containing string tables and character classification\n\n  Returns:\n    void - modifies output buffer and remaining size via pointers\n\n  Special Cases:\n    - Null terminator or zero remaining space causes immediate return\n    - Quote character (0x27) toggles literal string mode\n    - Character repeat count (1, 2, 3, 4) determines output format variant\n    - Character class 0x80 bit indicates multi-byte character type",
