      "name": "ValidateEmailFormat",
      "signature": "int ValidateEmailFormat(char * pEmailString)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "undefined4",
      "comment": "Validates email address format by checking for @ and . characters with proper positioning.\n\nThis function performs basic email format validation by ensuring the email address contains\nboth an @ symbol and a dot, with correct spacing requirements. It validates that there is\nat least one character before the @, at least one character between @ and ., and at least\none character after the dot.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if input string pointer is NULL - return 0 if NULL\n2. Search for @ character (0x40) in the string\n3. If @ not found, return 0 (invalid)\n4. Search for . character (0x2e) starting from @ position\n5. If . not found, return 0 (invalid)\n6. Verify at least one character before @ (string start < @ position)\n7. Verify at least one character between @ and . (@ + 1 < . position)\n8. Get full string length using CalculateStringLength\n9. Verify . is not at end of string (string length > . position + 1)\n10. Return 1 if all validations pass, 0 otherwise\n\nParameters:\n  pEmailString: Pointer to null-terminated email address string to validate\n\nReturns:\n  1 (true) if email format is valid; 0 (false) if invalid or NULL input\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - NULL input pointer returns 0\n  - Missing @ or . returns 0\n  - @ at string start (no local part) returns 0\n  - . immediately after @ (no domain) returns 0\n  - . at string end (no TLD) returns 0",
      "name_source": "LoD/PD2",
      "method": "NOP",
      "index": "NOP:55aa65a0e52a85d62e181468184658721001580355a54f744db9e74646a5ac16",
